<div id="content">
  <h3 class="linklist">1950s Books</h3>
  <div id="books"> 
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book001.jpg" alt="1st ed. Chilton Bolex Movie Guide" width="176" height="247"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex Movie Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">Kenneth Tydings</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 127 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Chilton Co. (1st edition, 1952)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">This book was part of the <i>Modern Camera Guide</i> series 
        published by Chilton, which focused on a particular manufacturer -- Bolex 
        motion picture cameras, in this case. It was written by Dr. Kenneth S. 
        Tydings, in collaboration with the Bolex Reporter staff, and contains 
        basic information on camera operation. Sections covering stereo movies 
        and color photography are included. It originally sold for $1.95 US </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book007.jpg" alt="1st ed. Bolex Guide" width="176" height="234"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">A. J. Surgenor</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 112 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Focal Press Ltd. (1st edition, 1953)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">Focal Press published a number of books in Britain dealing with a single make of camera, as part of their <i>Camera Guide</i> series. First published in 1953, the Bolex Guide is a fully illustrated book discussing the functions and operation of Bolex H-8 and H-16 cameras.
	  </li>
	  <li><strong>Cover scan:</strong> Courtesy of Helmuth Porschen.</li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book008.jpg" alt="1st ed. Bolex 8 Guide" width="176" height="235"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex 8 Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">G. R. Sharp</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 112 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Focal Press Ltd. (1st edition, 1956)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">This first edition of the Bolex 8 guide, published by Focal Press, offers operating instructions and discusses techniques for filming with Bolex pocket size cameras.
	  </li>
	  <li><strong>Cover scan:</strong> Courtesy of Helmuth Porschen.</li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book002.jpg" alt="Amphoto Bolex B8 C8 Guide" width="180" height="282"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex B8 - C8 Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">G. R. Sharp</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 96 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Amphoto, Focal Press (1957)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">The <i>Bolex 8 Guide</i> was later published in the United States by Amphoto; it offered a comprehensive look at 
        Bolex B-8, C-8 and B-8VS cameras. The book covers everything from simple 
        handling and operation of the camera, to advanced filming techniques, 
        special effects, film stock selection, animation, titling, editing and 
        accessories. As a guide book, it suffers from a lack of illustration, 
        but it does include depth of field charts for several lenses. </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book004.jpg" alt="Bolex 8mm Cine Guide" width="245" height="172"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex 8mm Cine Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">Robert Grosjean and Pierre Monier</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 100 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Paillard S.A. (1957)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> Multiple Versions</li>
      <li class="desc">The first official book on Bolex cameras was published 
        in French and later translated to English and German. The book contains 
        many cartoon illustrations and tips on using Bolex equipment, and was 
        mainly written with the amateur home movie maker in mind. Several useful 
        charts for the shutter speeds of different cameras and depth of field 
        tables for Kern lenses are included. </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book005.jpg" alt="Bolex 8mm US Version" width="245" height="173"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Your 8mm Cine Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">Robert Grosjean and Pierre Monier</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 100 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Paillard Inc., NY (1957)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">This version was intended for the US market and sold by 
        Paillard Inc, NY, as well as Bolex dealers throughout the country. This 
        is essentially the same book as the original Cine Guide, but printed with 
        a different cover. </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book006.jpg" alt="5th ed. Bolex H16 H8 Guide" width="176" height="236"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex H16-H8 Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">A. J. Surgenor</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 112 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Focal Press Ltd. (5th edition, 1958)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">Revised by E. A. Gilmour, the 5th edition of the <i>Bolex Guide</i> updates the content with sections on the H16 Reflex.
	   In order to 
        identify the early and late model H cameras, A.J. Surgenor groups them into "Series" based on changes made to the camera over time. </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
    <ul>
      <li><img class="fltleft" src="../images/ephemera/50book003.jpg" alt="8th ed. Bolex Movie Guide" width="178" height="282"></li>
      <li class="title"><strong>Bolex Movie Guide</strong></li>
      <li class="author">Kenneth Tydings</li>
      <li><strong>Paperback:</strong> 128 pages</li>
      <li><strong>Publisher:</strong> Chilton Co. (8th edition, 1959)</li>
      <li><strong>Language:</strong> English</li>
      <li class="desc">The Chilton Bolex guides were revised over the years to include 
        the latest cameras and equipment; the cover of the 8th edition in the series featured a B-8L illustration. It gives a general overview of equipment 
        and discusses several filming techniques and in-camera effects. Although this edition was published in 1959, no 
        camera or accessory later than 1957 is mentioned; it only briefly touches 
        on the H-16 Reflex and B-8VS, but makes no mention of the B-8L. </li>
    </ul>
    <hr class="spacer">
  </div>
